#edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200933 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200933 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200933 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200933 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200933 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200933 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e52abdf3-c0dc-5ef1-894f-dee194666a8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200939 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200939 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200939 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200939 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200939 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "90c7b183-e533-502b-8176-8388d1565702" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200940 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200940 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200940 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200940 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200940 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "17c3c1df-a7e2-5642-ad06-78f1c0578359" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200941 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200941 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200941 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200941 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186200941 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7af37f00-7de9-5a1f-b9ee-080fd67e4675" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203744 #edb/ref 1069 "e-15071" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203744 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor the trinomial $-4w^2-30w-50.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203744 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f57cd9ee-7613-5083-948a-2434078d6796" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203744 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n-4w^2-30w-50\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms in the trinomial have a common factor of $-2.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $-2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n-2(2w^2+15w+25)\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to factor the trinomial $2w^2+15w+25$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times 25 = 50$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($15$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $50.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $50$ are\n\n$$\n(1,50),\\quad (2,25),\\quad (5,10).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(5,10)$ add to $15{:}$ \n\n$$\n5+10 =15  \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $5w+10w,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{w}^{2}+15w+25 & = \\\\ 2{w}^{2}+5w+10w+25 & = \\\\ 2{w}^{2}+10w+5w+25 & = \\\\ (2{w}^{2}+10w)+(5w+25) & = \\\\ 2w(w+5)+5(w+5) & = \\\\ (2w+5)(w+5) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(2w+5)(w+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203745 #edb/ref 1069 "e-15088" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203745 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor the trinomial $-10t^2 - 35t + 20.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203745 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6a7913e-d53e-5885-b30c-87148e86032d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203745 #edb/ref 1074 "We must factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n-10t^2 - 35t +20\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $-5.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $-5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5(2{t}^{2}+7t-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $2t^2 + 7t - 4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times (-4) = -8$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($7$).\n\nThe factor pairs of $-8$ are\n\n$$\n(1,-8),\\quad (-1,8),\\quad (2,-4),\\quad (-2,4).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-1,8)$ add to $7{:}$ \n\n$$\n-1+8 = 8-1 = 7  \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-t+8t,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}+7t-4 & = \\\\ 2{t}^{2}-t+8t-4 & = \\\\ (2{t}^{2}-t)+(8t-4) & = \\\\ t(2t-1)+4(2t-1) & = \\\\ (2t-1)(t+4) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5(2t-1)(t+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203746 #edb/ref 1069 "e-15089" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203746 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor the trinomial $15x^2-40x+20.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203746 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "81afbe63-1358-5d90-999d-4797f5f44f78" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203746 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n15x^2-40x+20\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $5.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(3{x}^{2}-8x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $3x^2 - 8x + 4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $3 \\times 4 = 12$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient (which is $-8$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the constant is positive, we need to consider the *negative* factor pairs of $12.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $12$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-12),\\quad (-2,-6),\\quad (-3,-4).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-2,-6)$ add to $-8{:}$ \n\n$$\n-2-6= -8  \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-2x-6x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-8x+4 & = \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2x-6x+4 & = \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-6x-2x+4 & = \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-6x)-(2x-4) & = \\\\ 3x(x-2)-2(x-2) & = \\\\ (3x-2)(x-2) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(3x-2)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203942 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203942 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203942 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203942 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203942 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203942 #edb/ref 1039 "Quadratics With Positive Coefficients (After Initial Factoring)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203942 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186203744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203942 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "caf03377-f06d-5831-80b8-dcbf4978a8db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203943 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203943 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203943 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203943 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203943 #edb/ref 1039 "Quadratics With Negative Constants (After Initial Factoring)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203943 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186203745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203943 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b49cf980-1078-593b-bf56-c9a132c41273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203944 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203944 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186204337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203944 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186508106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203944 #edb/ref 1039 "Quadratics With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants (After Initial Factoring)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203944 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186203746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186203944 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0e1a8957-9964-5eba-b5f4-b0e1dd525838" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204138 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186204741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204138 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186204742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204138 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186204743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204138 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186204744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204138 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204138 #edb/ref 1045 "Further Factoring Trinomials With Leading Coefficients" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204138 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186204741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204138 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204138 #edb/ref 1050 900.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204138 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "534c3fc9-4831-5fca-ad59-09b388796c6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204336 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204336 #edb/ref 1069 "q-225002" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204336 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204336 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor $6{x}^{2}+26x+8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204336 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6a1a0c7d-b300-5f97-894e-7b4ae6893dfe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204336 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}+26x+8\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $2.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $2:$\n\n$$\n2(3{x}^{2}+13x+4)\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the trinomial $3{x}^{2}+13x+4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($3\\times 4=12$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($13$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $12.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $12$ are\n\n$$\n(1,12),\\,(3,4),\\,(6,2).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(1,12)$ add to $13:$ \n\n$$\n1+12=13\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $x+12x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+13x+4= \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+x+12x+4 & = \\\\ (3{x}^{2}+x)+(12x+4) & = \\\\ x(3x+1)+4(3x+1) & = \\\\ (x+4)(3x+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+4)(3x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204337 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204337 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204337 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186200941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204337 #edb/ref 1069 "q-225073" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204337 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204337 #edb/ref 1071 "**Fully** factor the following trinomial.\n\n$8{y}^{2}-36y+40=$\n{{field-1}}$($\n{{field-2}}$y-5)(y\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204337 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98b7e6f2-e40d-535e-a12b-3e5bd59b4957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204337 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n8{y}^{2}-36y+40\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $4.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2{y}^{2}-9y+10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $2{y}^{2}-9y+10$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $2\\times 10=20$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient (which is $-9$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the constant is positive, we need to consider the *negative* factor pairs of $20.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $20$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-20),\\,(-2,-10),\\,(-4,-5).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-4,-5)$ add to $-9:$ \n\n$$\n-4-5=-9\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-4y-5y,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-9y+10 & = \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-4y-5y+10 & = \\\\ (2{y}^{2}-4y)-(5y-10) & = \\\\ 2y(y-2)-5(y-2) & = \\\\ (2y-5)(y-2) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{y}^{2}-36y+40 & = \\\\ 4(2{y}^{2}-9y+10) & = \\\\ 4(2y-5)(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204741 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204741 #edb/ref 1061 178290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204741 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "97ca27d9-5627-500f-b4ce-94d3d62acc77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204741 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186205291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204742 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204742 #edb/ref 1061 178291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204742 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "53a8db76-c802-563a-80d3-ee07f4085259" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204742 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204743 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186204744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204743 #edb/ref 1061 178295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204743 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "408a8b32-052b-5f8b-9893-3e4c9e92fd6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204743 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204744 #edb/ref 1061 178299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204744 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "73b63fbf-6b48-571e-9ab0-6b574e48cf7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186204744 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186203944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205291 #edb/ref 1064 "When factoring a trinomial, we should always begin by factoring out any constant factors. Then, we factor whatever quadratic remains using the techniques we've studied. \n\nFor example, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\n6y^2+21y+9\n$$\n\nThe first thing to notice is that all the terms have a common factor of $3.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n3(2y^2 + 7y + 3)\n$$\n\nSo, our goal now is to factor the trinomial in parentheses:\n\n$$\n2y^2 + 7y + 3\n$$\n\nTo do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times 3 = 6$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($7$).\n\nAlso, since all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the *positive* factor pairs of $6.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $6$ are\n\n$$\n(1,6),\\quad (2,3).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(1,6)$ add to $7{:}$ \n\n$$\n1+6 =7  \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $y+6y,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}+7y+3= \\\\ 2{y}^{2}+y+6y+3 & = \\\\ (2{y}^{2}+y)+(6y+3) & = \\\\ y(2y+1)+3(2y+1) & = \\\\ (y+3)(2y+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}^{2}+21y+9 & = \\\\ 3(2{y}^{2}+7y+3) & = \\\\ 3(y+3)(2y+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nWhenever the coefficient of the quadratic term is negative, we should always factor this out first. That way, we're left with the task of factoring a trinomial containing a positive quadratic coefficient. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205291 #edb/ref 1065 9456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205291 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "35b6c679-c9c6-5b44-8de5-21106bc70674" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186205291 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507613 #edb/ref 1080 "2(w+4)(2w+1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507613 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507613 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0affc26b-60f0-51d5-b33d-6f529043658e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507621 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507621 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 1759