17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508003 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508003 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508003 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8e07d7d3-1dca-5770-910c-195812db5214" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508013 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508013 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508013 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508013 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508013 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b52fda95-06a0-5c0b-8089-003053de76c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508028 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508028 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508028 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508028 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508028 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186507841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508028 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508028 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508028 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508028 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f08665b6-f5cb-5dcf-be72-bcce09476082" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508054 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508054 #edb/ref 1069 "q-225283" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508054 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508054 #edb/ref 1071 "$4{w}^{2}-30w-16=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508054 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "30760314-ac6d-51dc-a95d-a6b32fc33073" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508054 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n4{w}^{2}-30w-16\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $2.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2{w}^{2}-15w-8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $2{w}^{2}-15w-8$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2\\times -8=-16$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($-15$).\n\nThe factor pairs of $-16$ are\n\n$$\n(1,-16),\\,(-1,16),\\,(2,-8),\\,(-2,8),\\,(4,-4).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(1,-16)$ add to $-15:$ \n\n$$\n1-16=-15\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $w-16w,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{w}^{2}-15w-8 & = \\\\ 2{w}^{2}+w-16w-8 & = \\\\ (2{w}^{2}+w)-(16w+8) & = \\\\ w(2w+1)-8(2w+1) & = \\\\ (w-8)(2w+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(w-8)(2w+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508073 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508073 #edb/ref 1069 "q-225265" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508073 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508073 #edb/ref 1071 "$-8{x}^{2}-30x+8=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508073 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "78ded0e2-d300-5bb0-ad2a-e4c3015aff51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508073 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n-8{x}^{2}-30x+8\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $-2.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(4{x}^{2}+15x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $4{x}^{2}+15x-4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $4\\times (-4)=-16$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient (which is $15$).\n\nThe factor pairs of $-16$  are\n\n$$\n(1,-16),\\,(-1,16),\\,(2,-8),\\,(-2,8),\\,(4,-4).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-1,16)$ add to $15:$ \n\n$$\n-1+16=15\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-x+16x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+15x-4 & = \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-x+16x-4 & = \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-x)+(16x-4) & = \\\\ x(4x-1)+4(4x-1) & = \\\\ (x+4)(4x-1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(x+4)(4x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508106 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508106 #edb/ref 1069 "q-225155" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508106 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508106 #edb/ref 1071 "$-50{x}^{2}+50x-8=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508106 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ded8bac7-351c-5c3f-9827-3494bc9b4c87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508106 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n-50{x}^{2}+50x-8\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $-2.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(25{x}^{2}-25x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $25{x}^{2}-25x+4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $25\\times 4=100$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient (which is $-25$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the constant is positive, we only need to consider the *negative* factor pairs of $100.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $100$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-100),\\,(-2,-50),\\,(-4,-25),\\,(-5,-20),\\,(-10,-10).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-5,-20)$ add to $-25:$ \n\n$$\n-5-20=-25\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-5x-20x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{x}^{2}-25x+4 & = \\\\ 25{x}^{2}-5x-20x+4 & = \\\\ (25{x}^{2}-5x)-(20x-4) & = \\\\ 5x(5x-1)-4(5x-1) & = \\\\ (5x-4)(5x-1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(5x-4)(5x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508114 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508114 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508114 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508114 #edb/ref 1069 "q-225007" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508114 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508114 #edb/ref 1071 "**Fully** factor the following trinomial.\n\n$6{u}^{2}+34u+20=$\n{{field-1}}$($\n{{field-2}}$u\\,+2)(u\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508114 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f09d890b-8768-5b87-b720-b755f6cef560" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508114 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n6{u}^{2}+34u+20\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms in the trinomial have a common factor of $2.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $2:$\n\n$$\n2(3{u}^{2}+17u+10)\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to factor the trinomial $3{u}^{2}+17u+10$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($3\\times 10=30$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($17$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $30.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $30$ are\n\n$$\n(1,30),\\,(2,15),\\,(3,10),\\,(6,5).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(2,15)$ add to $17:$ \n\n$$\n2+15=17\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $2u+15u,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2}+17u+10 & = \\\\ 3{u}^{2}+2u+15u+10 & = \\\\ 3{u}^{2}+15u+2u+10 & = \\\\ (3{u}^{2}+15u)+(2u+10) & = \\\\ 3u(u+5)+2(u+5) & = \\\\ (3u+2)(u+5) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{u}^{2}+34u+20= \\\\ 2(3{u}^{2}+17u+10)= \\\\ 2(3u+2)(u+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
